# CLAUDE.md — starter template for a repo run by the task loop
#
# Copy the sections you want into your repository's CLAUDE.md and edit the
# placeholders. Every rule here exists to make unattended + interactive
# sessions compound instead of thrash; delete what doesn't fit your project.

## Project

<one paragraph: what this repo is, how to run it, how to test it>

- Test command: `<pytest / npm test / ...>`
- Working branch: `<main / dev>` — commit with an explicit pathspec and a
  clear message; never bare `git add -A`.

## Mandatory workflow (research → plan → execute → verify)

- **Research before editing.** Read the affected module (not just the file
  mentioned), grep for the pattern across the repo, trace who calls it.
  For bug fixes: identify what input produces the wrong output and what
  OTHER inputs hit the same path.
- **Plan before executing.** State: root cause, affected files, changes per
  file, how you'll verify, what could regress.
- **Verify before claiming done.** Run the tests. Check the general case,
  not just the reported example. If any of these is true you made a
  band-aid: you added a special case; you fixed only the reported path; you
  only adjusted a constant.

## Session discipline — CONTINUOUS

- **Finishing the asked task is the midpoint, not the end.** After it
  ships: (1) fix the issues you found along the way (same session, separate
  commits), (2) then pull the next item from the task queue (`task-list`)
  and keep working. Stop only when blocked on a human decision.
- **Every discovered-but-unfixed issue leaves the session RUNNABLE, never a
  bare note:** `task-add "<concrete goal>" --repo <this repo>`. A concrete
  goal beats an inferred one; the stop-hook will nudge you if you forget.
- **File follow-ons as you work** (cap ~3/session): a verified adjacent
  improvement, a hygiene/cleanup item, or a reusable-script candidate each
  becomes its own queue item with `--hint "[followon] ..."`. Zero follow-ons
  is a fine outcome; only file work you verified is actually missing.
- **Leave the tree green.** If you hit a failing test or broken build — even
  one you didn't cause — fix it before you stop. A failure at committed HEAD
  is yours the moment it blocks your work.

## Delegation & context economy

- **Broad searches go to the `scout` agent**, not your own context — you
  want conclusions, not file dumps.
- **Fan-out work uses the wf-* agent leaves** (finder/verifier/judge/
  critic/dedup/tester/fixer/synth) with explicit model tiers: cheap for
  sweeps, mid for judgment, best-model only for the final synthesis.
- **Code is ground truth.** Memory files, docs, and old handoffs are an
  index for finding things — verify against source before acting.

## Safety boundaries (hard-stops for unattended work)

Never do these without an explicit human go-ahead in a live session:
- spend money, or make purchasing/pricing judgments
- delete data; destructive git (force-push, reset --hard, history rewrites)
- send anything to an external service (chat, email, tickets, deploys)
- touch credentials, secrets, or production configuration

An unattended worker that reaches one of these reports status
`needs-human` with exactly what is needed, and stops.

## Cross-session coordination

- Announce claims and landings in the session mailbox
  (`session_mailbox.py post claim|landing "..."`) so parallel sessions
  don't collide; read recent messages before big changes.
- Overlapping claim? Coordinate, don't clobber — and never destroy another
  session's uncommitted work.
